# SEO rules

Audited against https://thestash.deals on 2026-09-22 (42 pages, 29 deals). Every rule below cites
the measurement that motivated it. Numbers are from that audit; re-measure before assuming they hold.

## The one thing to internalise

The Stash Deals is **not the seller**. Google requires that "the merchant has to be the seller of the
product in order to be eligible for merchant listing experiences", so merchant listing rich results
(the shopping-style price/availability treatment) are permanently out of reach — do not spend effort
chasing them, and never mark up the site as the seller to try.

The flip side is a rich result that merchants are *barred* from: Google restricts the **pros and cons**
appearance to editorial product review pages ("A merchant describing its own product can't use it").
An editorial deal curator is exactly the qualifying case. That is this site's structural SEO advantage.

## Structured data

- Only single-deal pages (`/deals/<slug>/`) may carry `Product` markup. Google supports product rich
  results only on pages focused on a single product; listing pages use `ItemList`. The build already
  splits this correctly — keep it that way.
- Keep `offers.seller` as the **merchant**, never The Stash Deals. The site does not transact.
- `Product` + `offers` without `review` or `aggregateRating` makes the Rich Results Test emit a
  warning. Prefer a nested editorial `Review` carrying `positiveNotes` / `negativeNotes`
  (`ItemList` + `ListItem`) over adding a fabricated rating. Never invent ratings the site did not collect.
- `image` is only *recommended* for product snippets, not required, so its absence does not block
  eligibility — but the site currently ships one `<img>` per deal page and it is the logo, which
  forfeits Google Images entirely. Treat product imagery as CTR/Images upside, not a correctness fix.
- `priceValidUntil` is derived as `posted + 30 days`. At audit time 0 of 29 offers were expired
  (deals posted 2026-09-06 to 2026-09-21), but a stale `deals.json` silently turns every offer
  invalid. If the board stops being refreshed, expire deals rather than serving dead offers.

## Indexability

- Exactly one hostname may be indexable. At audit time `gearclearance.mcdaniel.fyi` served
  byte-identical content with `index, follow` and a permissive `robots.txt`, relying only on the
  canonical to `thestash.deals`. Note this is **not** a code defect: `build()` already walks all of
  `dist/` and throws if any output file mentions the prototype host. It is a Cloudflare Pages
  custom-domain setting — fix it there by detaching the domain or redirecting it, not in the build.
- Canonicals must be absolute and point at the primary host. `SITE` in `build.mjs` is the single
  source of truth for generated markup; the guardrail block deliberately pins the literal expected
  host so a silent `SITE` change cannot ship. Update both together, and never emit a second host.
- Keep `404.html` on `noindex, follow` and out of `sitemap.xml` (currently correct: 41 URLs, 404 absent).
- Preserve the trailing-slash convention. Live behaviour is `308` from `/guns` to `/guns/`; every
  internal link should already point at the canonical slashed form so no hop is needed.

## Titles and descriptions

- Keep `<title>` under ~60 characters so it survives SERP truncation. **17 of 42 pages currently
  exceed it**, the worst at 92 chars, because the template is `<deal title> — <price> | The Stash Deals`
  and the 18-char brand suffix plus the price consume the budget. Shorten or drop the suffix on deal
  pages before lengthening anything else.
- Keep meta descriptions roughly 120–158 characters. 4 pages exceed 158 and 9 fall under 120.
- Titles and descriptions must stay unique per URL. Currently 0 duplicates across 42 pages — the
  build should keep it that way.

## Content and added value

- Google's thin-affiliation policy targets affiliate pages "where the product descriptions and
  reviews are copied directly from the original merchant without any original content or added
  value". Word count is explicitly *not* the test. The existing `why` copy (original price context,
  e.g. per-round maths against big-box counter prices) is the right kind of content — protect it.
- The exposure is the templated pattern, not the prose: median unique editorial per deal page is
  **19 words, about 10% of a ~174-word page**. Add value the way Google names it — price context,
  comparisons, explicit decision criteria, pros and cons — never filler to hit a word count.
- Never paste merchant marketing copy into `why`, `title`, or a description.
- A page that exists only to hold affiliate links, with nothing a reader would value if the links
  were removed, should not be published.

## Affiliate compliance (already correct — do not regress)

- Every outbound merchant link keeps `rel="sponsored noopener noreferrer"`. This is exactly Google's
  requirement for monetised links. The build asserts it, but **only on the home page** — category
  and deal pages are unchecked, so keep the rel intact in both card and deal-hero templates.
- The FTC affiliate disclosure stays on every page, including the fact that the site is not the
  seller and not an FFL.

## Discovery and growth surface

- Indexable content needs an indexable URL. The `used` and `police-trade-in` facets (6 and 3 deals)
  exist only as client-side filter chips, so that high-intent inventory has no landing page at all.
  Any facet worth filtering by is worth a crawlable URL.
- The site has 11 landing pages (10 aisles + curated) and no hubs below aisle level, while the data
  already carries 18 distinct merchants and calibers in deal titles (9mm ×3, 5.56 ×2). Build hubs
  from data the board already has rather than inventing thin pages.
- Only create a hub when it has real inventory and its own reason to exist. A near-empty or
  near-duplicate hub is a doorway page.
- `/curated/` currently states it is "a placeholder for editorial tips" and its 546 words are all
  card boilerplate. An editorial hub with no editorial content should either get real content or
  stay out of the index.
